# What must not cross from this machine into the image that exists to be a
# different machine.
#
# `docker/linux-verify.Dockerfile` copies the whole tree, because the suite needs
# the whole tree — `cases/` is a declared workspace, `tools/` is in the vitest
# include, and both `tools` suites enumerate files with `git ls-files`, so `.git`
# comes too. That makes this file load-bearing rather than housekeeping: without
# it the copy would carry a macOS-built `node_modules` into a Linux container.
#
# Nothing would obviously break. `yarn install` would find the tree already
# populated, the native binaries would be the wrong platform, and the failure
# would surface as a suite behaving oddly on Linux — which is *precisely* the
# finding this image exists to produce, and would be a fabrication of this file's
# absence. The one thing a portability check must never do is import the machine
# it is checking against.

node_modules
.yarn/install-state.gz

# Build output. `yarn build` runs inside the image; carrying a macOS `dist` in
# would let a stale artifact answer for a compile that never happened.
dist
*.tsbuildinfo

# Run artifacts, none of which are inputs.
.variance/
coverage/
docker/results/
cases/*/incumbent/
cases/*/test-results/
cases/*/storybook-changed/

# Agent worktrees, which are checkouts of this same repository.
.claude/worktrees/
